\section{Caching}{

The four functions that query an external API -- \code{\link[=get_network]{get_network()}} (STRING),
\code{\link[=genes_from_kegg]{genes_from_kegg()}} (KEGG), \code{\link[=find_kws]{find_kws()}} and \code{\link[=fetch_kw_accessions]{fetch_kw_accessions()}}
(UniProt) -- are memoised on disk, so a repeated call with identical
arguments is served from the cache instead of the network. Entries live in
\code{tools::R_user_dir("proteoSE", "cache")}, expire after 30 days, and are
pruned once the cache exceeds 512 MB. Use \code{\link[=proteoSE_cache_info]{proteoSE_cache_info()}} to see the
location and current size, and \code{\link[=proteoSE_cache_clear]{proteoSE_cache_clear()}} to empty it.

Caching requires the suggested packages \pkg{memoise} and \pkg{cachem}; if
either is missing the functions simply fetch every time. Set
\code{options(proteoSE.cache = FALSE)} before loading the package to opt out.
}

\section{Progress messages}{

Import, enrichment and network functions report what they are doing while
they run. The messages are on at an interactive prompt and off everywhere
else -- scripts, \code{knitr}, \verb{R CMD check} -- so nothing prints where it would
only be noise. Override with \code{\link[=proteoSE_verbose]{proteoSE_verbose()}}, or set
\code{options(proteoSE.verbose = TRUE/FALSE)} directly.

Because the four cached functions above are memoised, their bodies do not
run on a cache hit; the absence of a "Fetching ..." message is how you tell
a cached answer from a live one.
}

\section{Namespace conflict policy}{

\strong{dplyr wins every name collision.} Where another imported package exports a
name dplyr also exports, dplyr's version is the one bound in the proteoSE
namespace, and the other package's version must be called fully qualified
(e.g. \code{AnnotationDbi::select()}, \code{MSnbase::combine()}). Two consequences for
anyone editing \verb{R/}:
\itemize{
\item Never \verb{@importFrom} a name that dplyr also exports. Qualify the call site
instead. Relying on NAMESPACE ordering to break the tie is fragile — the
file is alphabetically generated, so the winner can flip when an import is
added.
\item The same applies inside strings that are later \code{parse()}d and \code{eval()}ed
(see the iSEE panel command blocks in \code{R/isee-panels.R}): they resolve
through this namespace, so a bare \code{select()} there means \code{dplyr::select()}.
}
